---
import Chapter from "../layouts/Chapter.astro"
import Aside from "../components/Aside.astro"
import Code from "../components/Code.astro"
import { setup } from "../lib/docs"
import { registry, release, releaseSource } from "../lib/release"
---

<Chapter
  title="Installation"
  part="Guide"
  kicker="Chapter 2"
  lede="Add a component to a GPUI app, one step at a time. Each step says what it writes and what you should see."
>
  <div class="text">
    <p>
      You need Rust 1.98 or newer and your platform's native build tools. On macOS that is Xcode's
      Metal toolchain. On Ubuntu, install GPUI's libraries first:
    </p>
  </div>
  <Code
    code="sudo apt-get install libfontconfig1-dev libxcb1-dev libxkbcommon-dev libxkbcommon-x11-dev libwayland-dev"
    lang="bash"
  />

  <section class="step" id="step-1">
    <h2 class="h2"><span class="step__num">1</span> Create the app</h2>
    <Aside>
      <p>
        <code>gpuicn new</code> does steps 1 through 5 in one command. The steps below show what it
        writes, for an existing app or for a setup you want to understand.
      </p>
    </Aside>
    <div class="text">
      <p>Create a crate and add the pinned dependencies to <code>Cargo.toml</code>:</p>
    </div>
    <Code code={"cargo new my-app\ncd my-app"} lang="bash" />
    <Code code={`[dependencies]\n${setup.dependencies}`} lang="toml" />
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Import GPUI types through <code>gpui_kit</code>, never through a second <code>gpui</code>
        dependency, so your views and the components share one set of types.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="step-2">
    <h2 class="h2"><span class="step__num">2</span> Install the CLI</h2>
    <Aside>
      <p>
        The CLI is a Rust binary on crates.io. It needs no Node.js and no checkout of this repository.
      </p>
    </Aside>
    <Code code={`cargo install gpuicn-cli --version ${release.slice(1)} --locked`} lang="bash" />
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Then, from the app root, write <code>gpuicn.toml</code> against the registry for the same
        release:
      </p>
    </div>
    <Code code={`gpuicn --registry ${registry} init`} lang="bash" />
    <div class="text">
      <p>The file it writes:</p>
    </div>
    <Code
      code={`version = 2\nregistry = "${registry}"\noutput = "src/ui"\n\n[theme]\nbase_color = "neutral"\nappearance = "system"`}
      lang="toml"
    />
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        The registry URL pins component source to {release}. Fixes made after that tag are not included
        until you change the URL.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="step-3">
    <h2 class="h2"><span class="step__num">3</span> Add a component</h2>
    <Code code="gpuicn add button" lang="bash" />
    <Aside>
      <p>
        The CLI keeps existing files. To replace one on purpose, run the command with
        <code>--overwrite</code>, and run it with <code>--dry-run</code> first to see which files would
        change.
      </p>
    </Aside>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        This writes <code>src/ui/button.rs</code> and <code>src/ui/theme.rs</code>, and creates or updates
        <code>src/ui/mod.rs</code> to declare them:
      </p>
    </div>
    <Code code={"pub mod button;\npub mod theme;"} />
    <div class="text">
      <p>Add <code>mod ui;</code> to <code>src/main.rs</code>. The files under <code>src/ui</code> are yours to edit.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="step-4">
    <h2 class="h2"><span class="step__num">4</span> Add the fonts</h2>
    <Aside>
      <p>
        The theme names Geist for text and Geist Mono for code. To use other fonts, load them in your
        app and change <code>fonts</code> in <code>src/ui/theme.rs</code>.
      </p>
    </Aside>
    <div class="text">
      <p>Download the pinned Geist files and their license into <code>assets/fonts</code>:</p>
    </div>
    <Code
      code={`mkdir -p assets/fonts\nfor file in Geist-Regular.ttf Geist-Medium.ttf Geist-SemiBold.ttf GeistMono-Regular.ttf; do\n  curl -fL "${releaseSource}/site/assets/fonts/$file" -o "assets/fonts/$file"\ndone\ncurl -fL "${releaseSource}/LICENSES/Geist-OFL-1.1" -o assets/fonts/OFL.txt`}
      lang="bash"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="step-5">
    <h2 class="h2"><span class="step__num">5</span> Open a window</h2>
    <Aside>
      <p>
        <code>ui::app::run</code> registers the Lucide icons and the fonts, installs the light and dark
        themes generated from <code>assets/theme.css</code>, and follows the system appearance.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/examples/hello.rs" download>Download this file</a>.</p>
    </Aside>
    <div class="text">
      <p>Replace <code>src/main.rs</code> with the starter:</p>
    </div>
    <Code code={setup.starter} />
    <Code code="cargo run" lang="bash" />
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        You should see a window with one button that counts its clicks. Switch the system appearance
        and the window changes with it. <code>gpuicn theme set</code> changes the base color, the radius,
        and the appearance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="updating">
    <h2 class="h2">Updating to a newer release</h2>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        A new release can change installed source. To take the changes, commit your own edits first, so
        the diff after the update shows only what the release changed.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Install the newer CLI with <code>cargo install gpuicn-cli --version &lt;new&gt; --locked --force</code>.</li>
        <li>Change the registry URL in <code>gpuicn.toml</code> to the same release tag.</li>
        <li>Preview, replace, then review:</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Code code={"gpuicn add button --dry-run --overwrite\ngpuicn add button --overwrite\ngit diff -- src/ui"} lang="bash" />
    <div class="text">
      <p>Reapply the edits from your earlier commits that you still need.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .step {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--head) + 1rem);
  }
  .step > :global(* + *) {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }
  .step__num {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--rubric);
    margin-right: 0.35rem;
    vertical-align: 0.2em;
  }
</style>
